---

## Context

Before the brain-configuration refactor, `aide_brain` resolved which backend was
wired by dispatching the host's `.mcp.json` brain entry through a package-side
registry of known connectors. The registry inferred connector identity, selected a
matching driver, and the driver composed the `instructions` prose from a per-backend
template embedded in the package. That model made the package the author of the
agent-facing words — the user had no way to change what the agent was told about
their brain without modifying the package's source.

The refactor settles a sharper architectural boundary: the user owns the brain
instructions. They are written verbatim in the hand-edited `## Prose` body of
`.aide/config/brain.aide`, a config file the host owns. The package's runtime job is
to deliver those words unchanged — not to compose, render, or interpret them. The
server's control flow reaches only as far as deciding which branch fired (the
`BrainState` statuses from `buildBrainState`); beyond that, it either reads the file
or names a recovery command. The backend registry is retired entirely — no code in
this package may infer connector identity or dispatch on a connector enum.

The `composeInstructions/` helper subfolder is gone because there is no
per-connector composition to do. The non-ok remediation strings are short, fixed
sentences; they live as top-of-file constants in the orchestrator because they are
part of the orchestrator's branching logic, not separable behavior. The single-
helper exception in the playbook's progressive-disclosure pattern applies —
splitting a handful of `if` branches and a handful of constant strings into a
sibling subfolder would add ceremony without payoff.

The `brain.aide` schema collapsed alongside the registry retirement. The user-owned
file's frontmatter now carries exactly `name` (a human-readable label) and
`mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` (consumed by sync). Removed fields — `connector`,
`rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools` — are not part of any consumer's contract. This
tool's `ok`-branch consumer of `parseBrainAide` cares about exactly one slice of
the parsed result: the verbatim prose body. The frontmatter shape change does not
expand this tool's surface; it shrinks the temptation surface for downstream
regression.

This tool is on-demand only — called by agents that need the brain mid-task, never
fired on every `/aide` boot. The boot probe (`aide_info`) already reports brain
precondition state at boot time using the same `BrainState` vocabulary. Firing this
tool at boot would duplicate that work and bloat every session with prose the agent
throws away if the task does not need the brain.

## Strategy

**Prose-from-disk, not prose-from-template, is the load-bearing decision.** The
user writes agent instructions in their own words, tailored to their own brain
connector. Two connectors need structurally different instructions — an Obsidian
brain says "read the entry file and follow wikilinks," a hosted knowledge base might
say "search the indexed database and follow backlinks via the API." No template can
bridge that shape difference without forcing both into a uniformity neither actually
has. Hand-written prose per connector with verbatim return at agent-time is the only
shape that scales without requiring connector authors to agree on a shared vocabulary.
This traces to the desired outcome that `instructions` on `ok` is byte-identical to
what the user wrote.

**The response shape is `{ status, instructions }` and nothing else.** The old
shape carried `backend: <id>` as a structured signal consumers could branch on
programmatically. Under the new contract, consumers branch on `status` alone —
`ok` means everything is healthy, every other status names a specific
user-recovery path. The wired brain is the user's descriptive `name` label inside
`brain.aide`; that label is for the human reading the file and for the agent
narrating in conversation, never a discriminant the tool result threads back into
the agent-facing surface. The tool deliberately does NOT include `name`,
`connector`, or `backend` in the response — including any of them re-creates the
identity-dispatch temptation the registry retirement was meant to delete, just on
the consumer side instead of the server side. Removing every such field
eliminates that temptation by construction.

**Fixed non-ok prose strings, not dynamic composition.** Every non-ok branch
returns a short, static sentence that names a specific terminal command or user
action. Each sentence is self-contained: an agent that reads it knows exactly what
to tell the user without needing to parse or interpret anything. Choosing fixed
sentences over dynamic composition keeps the non-ok surface testable — the test
asserts the exact load-bearing phrase (e.g. "npx aidemd-mcp init" for
`no-brain-aide`, "npx aidemd-mcp sync" for `no-mcp-entry` and `mcp-drift`) rather
than a generated string that could drift. Uniform remediation across all
unrecognized wirings means a user with an exotic connector sees the same
actionable surface as a user with a standard one.

**A defensive fallback guards the "instructions never empty" invariant.** The `ok`
branch calls `parseBrainAide` after `buildBrainState` reports healthy state; these
two modules have overlapping validation — if `buildBrainState` returns `ok`, the
parser should also return `kind: ok`. In the event that upstream contract is
violated (a race between file deletion and the two calls, or a future refactor that
relaxes `buildBrainState`'s file check), the tool falls through to the
`no-brain-aide` remediation prose rather than returning an empty `instructions`.
The fallback target shifts to `no-brain-aide` because the upstream-contract-
violation pattern this guard exists for is "buildBrainState says ok but parser
disagrees" — a missing-or-broken-file shape, which `no-brain-aide` is the natural
remediation for. (The previous codebase routed this fallback to `invalid-path`;
that branch is gone with `rootPath`.)

**Status vocabulary mirrors `buildBrainState` exactly.** The statuses are
forwarded unchanged from the state the detector returns — `BrainState` is the
single source of truth for which statuses exist and what they mean. Adding a new
state to `BrainState` flows through automatically; this tool only needs a new
branch when the parent intent adds new vocabulary. This spec deliberately does NOT
pin a count of states or enumerate the membership inline. Counting in the spec
guarantees drift the moment the parent changes, and enumerating duplicates a
contract that already lives in the type. The schema collapse that removed
`invalid-path` is the worked example of why the count had to come out — the prior
spec said "today, five states and four non-ok constants" and that line went stale
the moment `rootPath` was removed.

## Good examples

A project with a properly configured `brain.aide` and matching `.mcp.json` entry.
The user's `## Prose` body reads:

```
Your brain is an Obsidian vault. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by
their vault-relative path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries
across every note in the vault. The vault's entry file is `CLAUDE.md` at the
vault root — read that first; it carries wikilinks (`[[note-name]]`) you follow
to deepen context. When a wikilink looks relevant to your task, read it (depth 1)
and check that note's links too. Stay in scope; don't follow links into unrelated
topics.
```

The tool returns:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "Your brain is an Obsidian vault. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by\ntheir vault-relative path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries\nacross every note in the vault. The vault's entry file is `CLAUDE.md` at the\nvault root — read that first; it carries wikilinks (`[[note-name]]`) you follow\nto deepen context. When a wikilink looks relevant to your task, read it (depth 1)\nand check that note's links too. Stay in scope; don't follow links into unrelated\ntopics."
}
```

No `backend` field, no `connector`, no `name`. The `instructions` text is the
user's prose verbatim — no server-composed sentences, no template substitution. If
the user's prose contained a literal `${...}` character sequence (say, because
their instructions document their own templating), that sequence appears
unchanged.

A project where `brain.aide` is absent — the user has not run `aide_init` yet:

```json
{
  "status": "no-brain-aide",
  "instructions": "No `.aide/config/brain.aide` is present in this project. Tell the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp init` to scaffold the brain config file, or `/aide:brain config` if they want to be walked through it. Do not proceed with this task as if the brain were available — the file must exist before brain-dependent work continues."
}
```

A project where `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` disagree — the user edited
`brain.aide` and forgot to run sync:

```json
{
  "status": "mcp-drift",
  "instructions": "`.aide/config/brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` disagree about the brain entry. Tell the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` in their terminal — that is the only command that mutates `.mcp.json` from `brain.aide`. Then restart Claude Code. Do not attempt to patch `.mcp.json` from this session."
}
```

## Bad examples

A tool that runs a substitution pass over the prose before returning it:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "Your brain is at D:/notes/my-vault. Use mcp__brain__read_note..."
}
```

Here `${...}` was replaced with `D:/notes/my-vault` in the prose body before
returning. Violates the verbatim-prose invariant. The user's prose may legitimately
contain `${...}` sequences documenting a templating language, or may simply not use
substitution at all; the substitution engine has no authority over agent-facing
prose. It belongs only in `mcpServerConfig.args` at install time.

A tool that composes `instructions` from connector-keyed strings inside the package:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "Use mcp__brain__read_note to read CLAUDE.md at the vault root. That file is the brain's navigation contract — once you read it, the brain takes over."
}
```

This looks correct but the text was composed from an `obsidian` entry in a
package-side template map, not from the user's `brain.aide`. If the user's prose
says something different — because they customized their vault layout, added a
different entry file, or wired a non-Obsidian connector — the agent receives
instructions that disagree with the user's brain. The package authored the words;
the user did not.

A tool that returns a removed discriminant:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "name": "obsidian",
  "instructions": "..."
}
```

Or:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": "obsidian",
  "instructions": "..."
}
```

Either shape gives consumers a label they can branch on — re-introducing
connector-dispatch on the caller side even though the server side removed it. The
response carries `status` + `instructions` only; the user's `name` lives inside
`brain.aide` for the human reader, never on the wire.

A tool that reaches into the parsed config for non-prose fields on the `ok`
branch:

```ts
const result = await parseBrainAide(root);
if (result.kind === "ok") {
  return {
    status: "ok",
    instructions: `Connected to ${result.config.name}: ${result.prose}`,
  };
}
```

Concatenating `name` into the agent-facing prose blends two surfaces that the
schema deliberately keeps apart: the frontmatter is sync's input, the prose is the
agent's input. The user owns whatever framing they want around their prose body;
this tool's job is to deliver the body verbatim, not to wrap it in
package-authored sentences.

A tool that re-introduces an `invalid-path` branch:

```ts
if (state.status === "invalid-path") return { status, instructions: INVALID_PATH_PROSE };
```

`invalid-path` is no longer a `BrainState` value — `rootPath` was removed from the
`brain.aide` schema, so the package no longer has a path to validate. A branch
that handles it is dead code; an `INVALID_PATH_PROSE` constant is dead text.
Re-adding either is the schema-collapse regression in different clothing.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` -- parent root spec; established that `brain.aide` prose
  returns verbatim with no server-side substitution, that the response shape
  carries `status` + `instructions` only (no `backend`, no `connector`, no
  `name`), that the in-code backend registry is retired entirely, and that the
  `BrainState` vocabulary covers the full recovery surface (`ok`,
  `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`). Every outcome and strategy
  paragraph in this spec traces to the parent's brain-layer outcomes.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` -- sibling parser spec; defines the
  tagged-result contract (`kind: ok | missing | malformed-frontmatter |
  malformed-body`), the verbatim-prose guarantee, the never-throws invariant, the
  two-field frontmatter schema (`name` + `mcpServerConfig`), the explicit
  rejection of removed fields (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`),
  and the `interpolateArgs` partitioning that ensures substitution never reaches
  the prose body. The `ok` branch of this tool depends on the verbatim prose
  guarantee.
- `src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` -- sibling detector spec; defines the
  `BrainState` tagged union this tool forwards unchanged, the no-`backend`-field
  contract, and the no-registry invariant. The tool's branching logic is a
  direct consumer of this detector's output.
